The LLM side of the compute lease, on
f280b26983ad0fdb705a0d9ebf0503e76f2899b0 after the six-patch production series
and llama-vulkan-view-alias-deps.patch.

This host admits one active qwen-owned device workload. scripts/image-service.py,
scripts/physics-service.py, and scripts/geometry-service.py each hold
~/qwen-webui-state/vulkan-workload.lock with flock(LOCK_EX) across one job, and
at f280b269 llama-server holds nothing, so a chat turn, a model load, and an
image generation share the device. This patch makes llama-server a writer under
the same lock.

The claim covers the load as well as the pass. load_model uploads the weights
through common_init_from_params, the projector through mtmd_init_from_file, and
the draft context through common_speculative_init_from_params, so the open and
the acquire sit at the top of load_model, in that order, because the acquire
returns true while the descriptor is closed and an acquire ahead of its own open
would admit every load while reporting success. A warmup decode inside
common_init_from_params sits inside the same hold by construction. One device
step stays outside it: common/arg.cpp calls ggml_backend_load_all() while
parsing argv, ahead of load_model, which under GGML_BACKEND_DL=OFF registers the
statically linked backends and enumerates the device. That step allocates no
device memory and submits no graph, and the first allocation of either kind is
inside the hold.

The wait bound belongs to the caller rather than to the lease, so the two call
sites take two acquires. A model load has a caller that carries a refusal --
load_model returns false and the launch ends -- and the owner lock above this
one refuses at once with status 75, so an unbounded acquire beneath it would
turn that refusal into a stall: workload_lease_acquire_bounded polls
LOCK_EX | LOCK_NB every 50 ms under a deadline.
QWEN_GPU_COMPUTE_LEASE_WAIT_S names it in seconds the way image-service.py
names its own with QWEN_IMAGE_LEASE_WAIT_S, 300 by default and 0 for one
non-blocking attempt, and a value outside 0 to 86400 is refused by name with
the default used in its place. Polling bounds the wait and buys no
cancellation: server_queue keeps its running flag private with no accessor and
server.cpp's is_terminating is file static, so nothing reachable here reads the
termination such a loop would check.

A decode pass keeps the blocking acquire. server_queue::start_loop re-enters
callback_update_slots only when a task arrives or the process terminates, so a
pass that gave up on the lease would strand its request until unrelated traffic
woke the loop, and a chat turn waiting behind an image generation is what the
lease is for. server.cpp registers SIGINT and SIGTERM with sa_flags = 0, so no
SA_RESTART covers that block and a terminating signal returns EINTR, which is
the cancellation the bounded acquire cannot reach.

Lease coverage is admitted with inactivity sleeping disabled, and load_model
refuses the configuration that asks for both ahead of the acquire and of every
upload. server_context::start_loop passes params_base.sleep_idle_seconds * 1000
to server_queue::start_loop, whose should_sleep() returns false for a negative
interval alone, so sleeping is on at zero as well as above it and the field's
own "if >0" comment in common/common.h understates the range. What the refusal
avoids is a state this patch cannot report through: handle_sleeping_state
reaches load_model on the wake, and server_queue::on_sleeping_state takes a
void callback whose caller clears the queue's sleeping flag whatever the wake
did, so a wake refused for the lease would leave the queue admitting requests
against a destroyed context. Recoverable sleep and wake under the lease needs
that callback to carry a result, which is a queue-state transition of its own.
The value read is the resolved one: load_model assigns params to params_base,
start_loop reads that field, and server_res_generator reads it again for
bypass_sleep.

The lease is two-sided in time rather than tied to residency. An idle loaded
server reserves memory while the device executes nothing, so residency alone
holds nothing; server_context_impl::update_slots owns the transitions after the
load, taking the lease where its all_idle check finds a busy slot and releasing
it where the check finds none. Both acquires return true while the lease is
held, so the per-pass acquire is a no-op under a load-path hold and the release
trails the final decode by exactly one update_slots pass, because a non-idle
pass posts SERVER_TASK_TYPE_NEXT_RESPONSE and the pass that observes the idle
slots is the next one.

A host function returning is not device completion. llama_decode queues its
graph on the backend stream and returns, so workload_lease_sync_device calls
llama_synchronize on the target context and on the draft context where one
exists, and every release site runs it first: the idle transition in
update_slots, and destroy(), which synchronizes while ctx_tgt and ctx_dft still
name the contexts, frees, and releases last, because tearing a context down is
device work of this server's own. The synchronize sits at the ownership
transition rather than in the token loop, so an idle transition pays it once and
a decoded token pays nothing.

The release reports what the kernel confirmed. flock(LOCK_UN) fails before it
changes anything -- EBADF or EINVAL on the descriptor, never a partial release
-- so a failure leaves the lock held and workload_lease_held stays true to say
so. Clearing it would let the next acquire return true on an in-memory assertion
the kernel refused, which is the one state the lease exists to exclude; keeping
it costs fairness instead, since every other holder waits on a lock this process
still owns until the descriptor closes with the process. The idle transition
repeats on every pass, so workload_lease_release_failed latches the error line to
one.

tmux starts a session from its server's environment, so an operator's
QWEN_GPU_COMPUTE_LEASE_WAIT_S reaches the server only through
scripts/qwen-webui-control.sh's forwarded set, which names it beside the other
serving variables; the 300 second default applies without it.

QWEN_GPU_COMPUTE_LEASE names the file and QWEN_VULKAN_WORKLOAD_LOCK is read
beside it for one transition release, in that order, with
scripts/qwen-capacity-policy.sh exporting both over one path. An unset or empty
name leaves the descriptor closed and every lease call inert, which is the
state of every llama-bench, probe, and standalone harness in this tree. A named
path that cannot be opened fails the load, because a server that skipped the
lease silently would run graphs beside a generation while its log said the
invariant held. The descriptor is opened once with O_CLOEXEC, so nothing the
process spawns inherits the open file description flock binds to, and it is
reused across a destroy() that releases the lease without closing it.

A stall is logged while it lasts rather than after it. Each acquire tries
LOCK_EX | LOCK_NB first; on EWOULDBLOCK it writes the waiting line naming the
path, and the bounded one names its deadline beside it, before the wait rather
than after it. The acquire line carries waited_ms measured across the wait
alone. The armed line the open writes is what a harness reads to prove the
binary carries this patch at all, since an unpatched server handed a lease path
loads and answers exactly as one that skipped the lease would.

The process doing the work holds the lease. In router mode server.cpp leaves
ctx_server uninitialised -- load_model runs only in the non-router branch -- so
the router parent opens nothing. server-models.cpp snapshots environ into
base_env at server_models_routes construction and spawns each child with that
copy plus LLAMA_SERVER_ROUTER_PORT, so the names reach the child that owns the
context and the graphs verbatim. scripts/cuda-runtime-env.sh scrubs the
GGML_CUDA_*, display, and vendor names, so the lease names cross the exec
boundary untouched.

The whole block compiles on POSIX alone, guarded the way server.cpp guards its
own sigaction block, since sys/file.h and flock have no Windows equivalent.

The hunk offsets encode the post-series line numbers, so the patch applies after
llama-vulkan-low-priority, llama-no-cpu-fallback, llama-vulkan-duty-cycle,
llama-vulkan-runtime-submit-limit, llama-vulkan-submit-trace,
llama-router-tools-proxy, and llama-vulkan-view-alias-deps, in that order.

scripts/test-load-lease-coverage.sh reads this patch against the pinned source
and refuses a reach that leaves the load uncovered, the load path's own acquire
unbounded, the sleeping refusal absent, a release ahead of its synchronize, or a
release that clears the hold the kernel declined to give back;
evidence/lease-coverage/ carries the reading taken before the extension and
after it.

